1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:02,480
Welcome to Launched.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:10,480
I'm Charlie Chapman, and today I'm excited to bring you the creators behind the incredible camera app, Hollide, Sebastian DeWitt and Ben Sandofsky.

3
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:24,880
Guys, welcome to the show.

4
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:25,360
Cool.

5
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:26,320
Thanks for having us.

6
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:38,000
People have probably I've probably mentioned this app on the show multiple times, but this, along with Overcast, Hollide specifically, is an app that has lived in my dock since it came out.

7
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:48,880
I thought since I first got an iPhone, but I did the research, realized I must have gone a couple months before, whenever, after I switched to iOS, before you guys even released this.

8
00:00:49,040 --> 00:01:02,240
So it hasn't been the entire time I've had an iPhone, but it's basically a part of the iPhone for me, and so I'm super excited to talk to you guys about kind of the story that led up to this, because it's one of my most used apps.

9
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:03,520
Oh, that's awesome.

10
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:05,440
That's really, really flattering to hear that.

11
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:07,520
I'd love to, love to hear that.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,640
It's going back, I think that places you at having an iPhone 8 as your first.

13
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:13,920
iPhone 7?

14
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:18,160
It was in, well, the iPhone 7 was out, but I didn't buy a new one.

15
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,000
So it was technically an iPhone 6 Plus.

16
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:21,200
Ah, okay.

17
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:23,840
But yeah, it was right around that time.

18
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:27,040
And I bought an iPhone for the camera after we had kids.

19
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:35,440
And so playing around with the different camera apps was a major reason why I even got the app or got the phone in the first place.

20
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:41,120
And so HoloLife came out, I guess, right at the right time because I was very deep into experimenting.

21
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:42,400
And it was perfect for that.

22
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:43,120
Awesome.

23
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:45,040
Well, it's funny that it's also, yeah.

24
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:52,160
Well, we were also experimenting and making apps at the same time., it's like, wow, this is the right time to be making camera apps.

25
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:54,960
Like the cameras on the phones were getting really powerful.

26
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:59,360
And yeah, it's just like, so weird how it all kind of came together at the same time.

27
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:06,960
Normally what we do on this show is we kind of walk through the story of the person who made the app leading up to them making it.

28
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:10,880
But with two people, it's sometimes tricky because I have a tendency to get into the weeds.

29
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:13,280
So I'll try to keep it somewhat brief.

30
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:19,440
But I do want to talk about both of your careers kind of that led to you coming together for Hollide.

31
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:21,520
So we'll start with Sebastian.

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,560
The three questions I always ask are, where are you from?

33
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,920
Do you have a formal education related to what you're doing here?

34
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:36,480
And then what kind of, I guess, as briefly as you can, what kind of led you towards meeting up with Ben to start Hollide?

35
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:39,120
Yeah, those are great questions.

36
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:41,520
So yeah, hi, I'm Sebastian.

37
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,240
I'm actually a Dutch guy living in the United States now.

38
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:48,240
Sometimes my accent will come out, particularly on the th sounds.

39
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:50,480
So I'll say, duh, giving me away.

40
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:56,000
I grew up in a small rural town in the Netherlands and Netherlands, there it is.

41
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:02,080
And that's where I kind of started getting into the creative pursuits.

42
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:05,360
So I'm the design half of the team.

43
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:10,480
And I didn't, I had some semblance of a professional education.

44
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:19,440
I dropped out of high school to pursue going to art school in like the city closest to my little farm hamlet where I grew up.

45
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:28,880
And I spent about two years there kind of enjoying playing with photography and analog photography and movable type and drawing naked people.

46
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,360
But mostly just teaching teachers Photoshop.

47
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:34,000
I was a pretty autodidactic person.

48
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:38,640
I like to follow like tutorials online on like photoshopping and stuff.

49
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:40,480
And in our school, got my first Mac.

50
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:44,720
And that's kind of what got me super excited about user interface and icon design.

51
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:56,320
And started just kind of doing freelance work like that, which led me to start working with larger clients and basically finding out about UI design before it was a field that was taught in school.

52
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:00,960
And that brought me to becoming a professional user interface designer.

53
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:03,520
So I worked for companies like Apple for a bit.

54
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:12,800
Mozilla freelanced for companies like Sony and EA and a bunch of other companies until I was doing a stint of freelancing.

55
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:23,760
and at one point Ben kind of slid into my Twitter DMs around it was WWDC and Apple had just announced that they were going to create a set of APIs.

56
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:30,960
I think during a State of the Union they announced this, not like the main keynote, but they were going to create a set of APIs to give more fine grained control over the camera.

57
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:42,480
And Ben and I were acquaintances through Twitter and kind of we both had an absolute passion for photography and for apps as well.

58
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:49,600
And yeah, he he sent me a DM and it was like, hey, do you want to meet up and talk about maybe like making a video camera up?

59
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,080
And well, as they say, the rest is history.

60
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:53,120
Wow, okay.

61
00:04:53,760 --> 00:05:01,920
I was expecting like there was a bunch of other things before that, but it sounds like Hollide was the thing that kind of brought you together, really.

62
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:02,480
Yeah.

63
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:07,520
So I guess before we dive too deep into that, let's do the same three questions for Ben.

64
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:16,480
So where are you from, formal education related to what you do, and then what was your career up to you slipping into Sebastian's DMs?

65
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:18,800
Sure, sure.

66
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:27,600
So yeah, I grew up around the Boston area and I went to college for computer science and actually doubled majored in humanities.

67
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:32,480
And I wasn't really sure what I wanted to do with the computer science.

68
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:40,800
Like, I, you know, maybe I want to work in like computer animation or video games, but it turns out when you graduate, like, those jobs suck.

69
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:42,640
They're really hard to get.

70
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:48,480
And then if you have, if you have student loans, they aren't the best way of paying back student loans.

71
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:52,240
So, you know, I graduated, I worked at some large companies.

72
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:56,000
And then, you know, working in a large company has a lot of advantages.

73
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:57,520
So, you know, there's no shame in that.

74
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:38,240
You know, it's very reliable work, but it's not really wasn't really stimulating to me and so you know over the years I started like following what what's it like to build your own company and do your own startup and things like that and like I read you know posts by people like Joel Spolsky who had this great blog Joel on software and you know there's tons of people I'm sure you've covered on the show who talk about behind the scenes and so well basically like a lot of what your listeners are probably going through and I think at that time I tried doing my own startup which like my I want to say startup it's like bootstrap thing it was like it was like a website I knew a bunch of actors who needed to learn how to or they needed to manage their own promotional sites.

75
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:48,560
And so I was like, oh, what if I build like a SaaS product that will help them manage their own sites so I don't have to work with flaky webmasters who charge like fifty bucks to update their headshots, right?

76
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:50,400
And so, yeah, it failed.

77
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:51,680
It failed miserably.

78
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:54,400
And this was like, okay, maybe I'm not good at that.

79
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:56,560
Maybe I'm not good at building my own products.

80
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:58,640
But it was, I want to say, a learning experience.

81
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:05,920
And so after that, I was like, okay, well, if I want to work on something I at least love, then what are some of the products that I love?

82
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:07,920
And maybe I can go work for one of those companies.

83
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:14,560
And so at the time, I was spending all this, all my time on this website with a cute little blue bird.

84
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:17,520
And it was like really sucking away my whole day.

85
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:19,600
Like, wow, I can't put this down.

86
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:25,840
And so I applied and I applied right on the website and I went to work for Twitter, uh, 2009.

87
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:27,600
And so, yeah.

88
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:34,240
From there until like right after the IPO, I was there and I got to see, okay, this is how startups work.

89
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:36,000
This is how the sausage is made.

90
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:37,920
This is how you iterate on products.

91
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:42,000
And so it was a super insightful experience.

92
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:45,600
And then, you know, when I got out of there, okay, what do I want to do now?

93
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:56,400
Uh, no way do I want to go through like these other startups that you get offered, like, you know, pretty much everything else that came up as far as startups, every single offer I got was the companies no longer exist today.

94
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:56,800
Right?

95
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:58,240
So I dodged that bullet.

96
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:01,040
But I'm like, well, maybe I want to try doing my own thing again.

97
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:01,360
Right?

98
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:06,080
Maybe I figured out enough about what I'm, how you make products and figure it out.

99
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:11,920
And so, yeah, I just made a list of different things I want to iterate on or different products I want to build.

100
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:17,280
And I kept returning to this camera app and because I've been really getting into photography again.

101
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:19,920
And this was in, this was in 2009?

102
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:21,680
Oh, this was 2014.

103
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,680
So this was after my release on Twitter.

104
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:25,200
So, yeah, yeah, yeah., yeah, yeah.

105
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:28,880
So I was like at that point, I was like, okay, what do I actually want to do?

106
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:31,360
And then I kind of returned to, you know, photography.

107
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:37,360
And then what I studied in college, like computer graphics was a really cool thing, but it was so hard to work for other people.

108
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:40,960
You know, maybe if I could do anything, maybe I'd do something with computer graphics.

109
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:45,200
And so the camera app, sort of the overlap and the Venn diagram between that.

110
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:50,480
And there were like some small things around camera apps that like suggested they might be monetizable.

111
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:56,240
Like most of the time when people are like, yeah, I knew this product would work is like a justification afterwards.

112
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:58,000
Like, ultimately we were lucky.

113
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,880
I think that camera apps ended up being monetizable.

114
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:02,640
But there were some things in the camera.

115
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:05,760
There was like, okay, maybe this will be easier to make money by building an app.

116
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:08,880
But honestly, at the time, everyone was like, yeah, there's no way to build.

117
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:11,200
There's no way to make any money building apps, right?

118
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:12,400
The app store's dead.

119
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:14,160
You have to work for someone else.

120
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:16,240
And so, you know, we were really surprised.

121
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:23,440
I think we met up and then I think it was a year later when we finally launched and like, oh, hey, okay, this is going way better than we expected.

122
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:30,000
I thought it was just going to be like a calling card where I could get some contract work doing graphics programming and it just kind of took on a life of its own.

123
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:33,680
So what made you contact Sebastian then?

124
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:37,680
Why do you think he might be a good partner to try and bring this thing to life.

125
00:09:39,200 --> 00:10:00,240
So he had just published his two year long review of the Leica M eight, which for the people who aren't photographer, hardcore photography people, it's like, it's a really renowned camera, and it's, you know, it's a really, it was a really deep, insightful view of the camera for someone who is super passionate about photography.

126
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:13,920
And so, you know, there's the joke that finding the right cofounder is like dating, where like, you need to find someone who also, like, they're compatible with your outlook, and I'm sure there's tons of people you'll meet, they may have a different view on, like, perfection.ism in the product.

127
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:22,000
And so, you know, I think it seemed as though he checked all the boxes as far as how he looked at products, but also his passion around photography.

128
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:28,000
And also it didn't hurt that he was in San Francisco and there was a coffee shop halfway in between us.

129
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:32,240
So, I don't know, I don't know what you were thinking, Sebastian, when I slid into your DMs.

130
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:37,200
Yeah, it was really fateful, honestly.

131
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:45,040
I think I really wanted to create something that was really truly my own or like aligned with my vision.

132
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:48,000
And it turned out me and Ben had a lot of really similar ideas on it.

133
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:57,200
And Ben really hits the nail on the head when he said that we didn't anticipate, like, starting we weren't like, oh, we're going to be founders and like start a business.

134
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:02,800
This was like, hey, you want to build an app that we think is cool and maybe ten other people will use it too?

135
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:04,080
Yeah, let's do it.

136
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:09,520
Like, we I just thought we would just create it and completely for scratching our own edge.

137
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:13,040
Because my mother was actually visiting when we launched it.

138
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:17,040
And when I told her what I was working on, we thought, this is classic Dutch directness too.

139
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:21,040
But she actually said, like, aren't there just a lot of camera apps already?

140
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:23,920
Not like, oh, I'm so happy for you.

141
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:24,560
That's really exciting.

142
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:27,360
No, just like, good luck with that.

143
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:30,160
And that's kind of how a lot of people responded to it.

144
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:32,560
But yeah, that surprised us.

145
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:40,640
The initial, like, approach then was you kind of want to, you're, you weren't trying to build a business.

146
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:43,600
You weren't necessarily thinking business models necessarily upfront.

147
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:51,120
It was really more like, you want to make something that you're proud of and kind of put your stamp on the world with this thing.

148
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:53,280
Is that an accurate portrayal?

149
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:55,600
Yeah, I think so, Ben.

150
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:03,200
Yeah, I mean, we're careful not to choose something that clearly has no business model that could work.

151
00:12:04,080 --> 00:12:09,200
It had a chance of working, but I think our expectations were really, really low.

152
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:16,480
And it was more about, for me, it was like, I mostly worked for other people and didn't have something with my name attached.

153
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:21,840
So my viewpoint was like, this was, this was really about an exercise and having something I could be proud of.

154
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:22,080
Yeah.

155
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:25,120
If I get beer money, then that's great.

156
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:25,920
That's wonderful.

157
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:35,680
I think that's the reason I bring that up is I do think that, especially at the beginning, really colors a lot of small decisions you make along the way though.

158
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:56,560
And at least the initial version of Hollide, it really did feel like it was a, I mean, I guess Apple products give me a similar vibe, and obviously those are very much business decisions, but they have this sort of like opinion about the world, despite what the world maybe necessarily wants.

159
00:12:56,720 --> 00:13:02,800
It wasn't built to solve the world's problem, it was built to like express the creator's values.

160
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:10,000
That sounds really high fallouting, I guess, but Hollide is unique.

161
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:11,680
And I guess let's maybe dive into that.

162
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:24,640
So once you decided to make a camera app, what was the initial driver for how you could put your stamp on the market that, as you said, was already super full of other camera apps?

163
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:39,120
I think one of the things I realized very early on working with Ben, and it's always different, right, when a designer and an engineer work together, it can be anything from really magical collaboration to a really frustrating process.

164
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:42,880
And we have the exceptional privilege that we work together really well.

165
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:46,000
So we just have kind of an understanding of what we value.

166
00:13:46,160 --> 00:13:50,640
I don't think we really had any, ever had any product conflicts and like we should do this or do that.

167
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:57,440
Like we just kind of feel like we both have ideas that we really appreciate and really feel like we have an equal contribution in that sense.

168
00:13:57,600 --> 00:13:58,320
And that's really nice.

169
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:01,600
And we just wanted things to kind of find a middle road.

170
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:07,680
Like we were not happy with the camera app because it didn't give us enough control, but we also weren't happy with what was out there.

171
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:18,000
That thing my mother saw where there was a ton of camera apps out there, those took an approach that kind of, to me at least, not to trash talk anyone, just resembled a kind of kitchen sink approach.

172
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:18,880
Like it just does everything.

173
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:30,960
And I think to this day, like one of the first, like the moment we released the app, like one of the top requestsests, which is still statistic, is that we don't have digital zoom, which the built-in camera has, and I'm sure a lot of other apps have.

174
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:35,280
But that's like one of those examples of, that's just the way we designed it in an opinionated way.

175
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:40,240
We think digital zoom is pointless because it crops your image and throws away the rest of the picture.

176
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:42,480
So we decided to just never offer that.

177
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:44,800
It just didn't work for the way we take photos.

178
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:49,120
And if we want to zoom it, we could zoom it later, you know, and then throw away part of the shot.

179
00:14:50,000 --> 00:15:01,280
Although you just released a feature that sort of leans in that direction, but still it sounds like it fits within your vision, which is the macro mode has a digital zoom, right?

180
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:05,440
But it's specific for that way of taking photographs.

181
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:12,800
I know we're jumping ahead, but I'm curious how that came about then, if you were sort of opposed to digital zoom in your design.

182
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:14,880
How did you land there with that?

183
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:22,240
Yeah, this one in particular, like, combines zoom with machine learning based detail enhancement.

184
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:24,400
So it's not quite the same thing.

185
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:32,240
But the idea was that we would have, for instance, like the interface of Halad itself is a camera that's very much powered by gestural interaction.

186
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:36,640
So swiping up and down can adjust exposure really quick and swiping left and right you can expose.

187
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:38,240
You can exposure, you can adjust the focus.

188
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:46,800
We found that simplifying that sort of gestural grammar would really benefit the camera, and zoom didn't really fit in that.

189
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:56,320
So that was just kind of like the way a lot of people expect cameras to work, just going a different way because that's the way we wanted it to be, and that I think is kind of a founding principle.

190
00:15:57,200 --> 00:16:04,880
We go our own way, we have our opinionated way of doing things, and it lands somewhere between on the complexity scale of camera apps.

191
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:10,640
Like it, to us, fits in so snugly into that little sort of Goldilocks zone there.

192
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:12,000
Ben, what do you think?

193
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:16,160
Yeah, and like, so digital zoom is a perfect example there.

194
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:21,040
We I would say that we there's an API you can just turn on and be like, yeah, we'll crop your photos.

195
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:26,160
And, you know, we chose not to because when we decided we wanted to do it, we wanted to do it right.

196
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:33,200
So in the case of our macro mode, we we keep an unedited version of the image.

197
00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:40,400
So if you don't like the crop, we didn't throw out the data, which, again, I don't play with other camera apps that much.

198
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:42,800
I think we're the only app that does this.

199
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:47,360
But just as photographers, like philosophically, there's no point throwing out data.

200
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:50,800
Every photographer edits the photo later, so we're going to keep it around.

201
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:54,960
And so, you know, it's looking at our roadmap, adding that feature.

202
00:16:55,200 --> 00:16:58,240
It sort of fits in, okay, what are some other features we're going to be building in?

203
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:02,480
And this is sort of like a chess piece move, okay, we this helps us with the plumbing for this.

204
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:04,480
This will lead to, you know, X, Y and Z.

205
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:09,200
So it's really about like focusing on the company and what we want to be working on.

206
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:14,160
And so we decided, okay, this is the right time to start tackling what is our alternative to digital zoom.

207
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:21,680
And on the flip side, I'll also say like opinions are important, but you don't want to be so myopic that you're ignoring like accessibility issues.

208
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:24,160
For example, some people use digital zoom.

209
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:26,800
Digital zoom because it helps them focus.

210
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:38,080
So in Mark 2, which we launched, geez, a year and change ago, we added a focus loop which gives you the benefit of digital zoom if you're using it as a focus aid.

211
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:42,800
But it's not about cropping the image, it's distinctly solving that problem.

212
00:17:43,520 --> 00:17:46,480
And so it's very easy to be like, oh, you know, we're omakase.

213
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:52,960
We view our product and you have to recognize there are other people who are, who have different needs than you.

214
00:17:53,280 --> 00:17:59,920
And you need to still listen to your users because you can't, you know, you can't view everything through your own personal lens, right?

215
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:01,360
I like that though.

216
00:18:01,920 --> 00:18:11,280
If you're getting a bunch of requests for something that you don't think is right for the product, you can dive a layer deeper and look for the reason why people are asking for that.

217
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:17,600
And maybe you can still solve that same problem in a way that fits sort of the ethos of the app itself.

218
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:18,800
Yeah, exactly.

219
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:21,360
Yeah, I think it's a really good way to put it, Charlie.

220
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:34,880
And I think what one of the things me and Ben also felt really strongly about was, you know, he found me through that review in which I kind of like waxed poetic about how much I like this camera, which by every measurable standard is kind of a pain in the butt to use.

221
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,680
It's all, it's this, this, this hunk of brass made in Germany that is still kind of stuck in the past.

222
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:43,760
Like you have to, you can't even look through the lens.

223
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:44,640
It's all manual.

224
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:53,200
It's, you know, it's very unforgiving, but it has that tactical quality that has that, it has that camera magic.

225
00:18:53,360 --> 00:19:03,200
Like if you give, yeah, one of those old film cameras to like a child, you know, they'll play with the dials and the switches and the knobs because it just, it just feels so dang satisfying, you know?

226
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:03,920
It feels great.

227
00:19:04,160 --> 00:19:06,800
And we felt like maybe we can capture some of that.

228
00:19:07,040 --> 00:19:11,200
Like we didn't delude, like delude ourselves into thinking we could bring that level of the light.

229
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:12,320
That would be really, really hard.

230
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:16,480
But some of that delight would be really cool to bring into a camera app.

231
00:19:16,640 --> 00:19:24,640
And so that also kind of became our founding principle of, oh, how can we make it feel like really delightful and tactile and just kind of fun?

232
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:34,640
My favorite example of this, I don't know if this is in V one or not, but the fact that it has the little square that shows the level in the middle.

233
00:19:35,680 --> 00:19:43,360
And when you have it square, you get this little click, you know, with the amazing haptics that have been in iPhones for a while.

234
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:50,720
That, I don't know how many people I've given my camera to who probably don't care., but I'm all excited about it.

235
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:52,960
I'll hand my phone to you to be like, Do you feel this?

236
00:19:53,120 --> 00:19:54,240
Do you feel how awesome this is?

237
00:19:54,640 --> 00:20:18,160
Because I'll be taking the picture, you know, well, now especially with macro, like, upside down because the lens is on the top of the camera and I can't even really see the screen because I'm trying to get the specific angle, but I know I want this level and I can feel when it's right and then sit there and smash where I'm pretty sure the shutter is like forty times just to see if I can get the right thing.

238
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:24,960
But it's like this little satisfying tactile thing that also provides a whole bunch of extra energy.

239
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:26,560
That's great to hear.

240
00:20:26,640 --> 00:20:28,160
I think that's that's a Ben invention.

241
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:28,960
Oh, yeah.

242
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:38,080
Well, I think, well, you obviously designed the level and I think that the brainstorming around this feature was like, hey, wouldn't it be cool if this we tried this?

243
00:20:38,240 --> 00:20:45,280
And then I knocked out a like an ugly version or like a really hacky version to feel to get a feel for it in our hands.

244
00:20:45,440 --> 00:20:46,720
And like, okay, yeah, that's cool.

245
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:53,360
But I would say also, we're very lucky that we're both working on a product that we personally like.

246
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:54,800
We're both photographers.

247
00:20:55,680 --> 00:21:06,720
You know, Sebastian being a designer is at a different level than me, but like people who., you know, we know what it feels like to have a digital SLR or we know what it feels like to have the right product.

248
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:13,440
And so it's much harder, you know, if you're working on medical imaging software and you're not a doctor, like what does a doctor want?

249
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:14,000
Right.

250
00:21:14,560 --> 00:21:16,960
And so we're super lucky in that regard.

251
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:27,680
And so, you know, so, you know, so many of the ideas do come to us like just through improvisation or just going back and forth and eventually striking a balance.

252
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:36,720
But it's like there's so much to be said around like looking at what's you can't communicate in, um, if you toss a, uh, a sketch file over a wall to me.

253
00:21:37,280 --> 00:21:50,480
like I could build exactly pixel perfect everything Sebastian asks, but like there's always going to be like this middle ground where you need animations or you need haptics or you need to figure out the right type of transition between the different screens.

254
00:21:51,120 --> 00:22:04,640
And so there's a lot to be said for every engineer like learning like the very, very basics of design and having an appreciation for that because it just makes it so much easier to collaborate with a designer and it's just it's a force multiplier.

255
00:22:05,040 --> 00:22:05,120
Yeah.

256
00:22:05,680 --> 00:22:10,640
How do you validate design stuff or test design stuff like that?

257
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:16,240
Like do you have a big beta group that you're sending things to early and getting a lot of feedback?

258
00:22:16,880 --> 00:22:16,960
No.

259
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:20,560
Or a product.

260
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:29,840
With product, we just do whatever feels right, you know, while also getting opinions from people that we know and care about.

261
00:22:30,320 --> 00:22:42,240
But for the most part, like product direction, we say this is where we want to move the product and then Sebastian will create a design and then I'll knock something out really quickly and we'll get a feel for it internally.

262
00:22:42,560 --> 00:22:48,720
But for the most part, I'd say beta testers are all about finding bugs or, you know, obviously if we, we, we, it turns out.

263
00:22:49,280 --> 00:22:50,560
something we built is confusing.

264
00:22:51,120 --> 00:22:54,320
I would hope they tell us like or that this thing doesn't make sense.

265
00:22:54,640 --> 00:22:58,080
But for the most part it's about validation of what we've kind of already made as far as the product.

266
00:22:58,400 --> 00:22:59,680
We don't do any kind of analytics.

267
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:03,600
You know, we don't do anything like driven like that for the product.

268
00:23:04,320 --> 00:23:04,480
Yeah.

269
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:09,120
And to be fair, we're also somewhat ruthless when like stuff really doesn't work.

270
00:23:09,280 --> 00:23:14,240
Like the first version of Howlett had this extremely maybe I was like using online dating apps too much at the time.

271
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:28,080
It had this slightly too clever for its own good feature where instead of going through your images and it would behave like the normal camera role, you could swipe like up and down to go through your photos, but then swipe left or right to favorite them or to trash them so you could quickly review them.

272
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:30,160
I was like, yeah, quick review feature.

273
00:23:30,320 --> 00:23:32,240
And everyone was like, why does it work this way?

274
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:33,040
This is horrible.

275
00:23:33,200 --> 00:23:34,640
My muscle memory swipe this way.

276
00:23:34,720 --> 00:23:35,920
Now I'm deleting my photos.

277
00:23:36,160 --> 00:23:39,440
And we were just saying, okay, that's just been this.

278
00:23:39,840 --> 00:23:41,440
Yeah, I think I came up with that feature.

279
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:45,360
If we want to balance out the level thing, I also, I think I came up with that.

280
00:23:45,680 --> 00:23:46,560
Wouldn't this be cool?

281
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:51,520
And then I think we, like, eventually took us a few months to finally rip it all out.

282
00:23:51,600 --> 00:23:55,600
But yeah, I was like, okay, on paper it was, it was a great idea on paper, right?

283
00:23:55,840 --> 00:23:58,160
It was a good idea, but it just, some things don't work.

284
00:23:58,800 --> 00:23:58,880
Yeah.

285
00:23:59,520 --> 00:24:00,160
That's awesome.

286
00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:00,480
Okay.

287
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:01,520
Okay, that makes sense.

288
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:04,560
I was going to say that was actually my next question was when have you had to do that?

289
00:24:04,640 --> 00:24:09,440
Because I see this very linear path forward and I can't think of anything you've had to pull back on.

290
00:24:09,680 --> 00:24:12,960
But I forgot that that's how the gallery kind of used to work.

291
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:14,640
Yeah, it was not good.

292
00:24:16,640 --> 00:24:31,360
So one of the things that I feel like defines Hololide now in my head is there's a lot like I'm going to use the word color science and I'm assuming that's probably inaccurate somehow because I don't know enough about that world.

293
00:24:31,680 --> 00:24:38,080
But you do these awesome blog posts going really deep into the details on how you're doing it your processing and everything.

294
00:24:38,800 --> 00:24:47,760
Um, was that was that part of the first version or is that something that's sort of been enabled as you've gotten access to, like, deeper APIs from Apple?

295
00:24:48,560 --> 00:24:57,360
So part of the reason also for wanting to do this is having an excuse to go deep on, like, all these topics and like really understanding more about color spaces.

296
00:24:57,920 --> 00:25:02,480
Uh, similarly also a reason for the company is to have a tax write off when we want to buy camera gear.

297
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:06,320
Like, you know, there's so many reasons for a company.

298
00:25:06,480 --> 00:25:06,560
Yeah.

299
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:07,760
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

300
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:08,640
I need this new lens.

301
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:09,200
Um, no.

302
00:25:09,840 --> 00:25:16,000
Uh, so, so part of it's like for my own., like a lot of these posts I could have written in the first year.

303
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:17,600
I mean, ProRaw didn't exist.

304
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:23,040
And part of it's just like really fascinating doing deep dives around like lenses and things like that.

305
00:25:23,280 --> 00:25:30,640
But yeah, it's interesting how our product has evolved alongside Apple's movement toward computational photography.

306
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:39,680
So, you know, for example, with Mark II, in the first version of the app, it was all about the manual controls, you know, and having access to RAW.

307
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:47,040
And so in the first version, they were all, it was easy to switch over to manual mode because the controls were within thumb reach.

308
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:48,320
But we found the auto.

309
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:52,320
exposure algorithms kept improving from iPhone to iPhone.

310
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:58,480
And so there was a slight change in the product where you can still get to manual, but you have to reach in the upper right, right?

311
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:02,000
Because it's just less common that we find ourselves reaching for that feature.

312
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:07,600
So yeah, a lot of the product itself has been evolving as Apple gives us access to new APIs.

313
00:26:08,400 --> 00:26:09,520
Please give us night mode.

314
00:26:09,760 --> 00:26:11,520
We're asking for that every year.

315
00:26:12,480 --> 00:26:13,920
So we're not fully on parity.

316
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:21,680
But it's also, I have to really give credit to Apple that they, you know, their camera and imaging groups are so communicative.

317
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:24,480
And with lab sessions they've asked like, what.

318
00:26:25,520 --> 00:26:26,480
could we do to help you out?

319
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:32,400
And so we'll say, you know, could you add access to this particular API so that we can give users more control.

320
00:26:33,040 --> 00:26:38,320
And so now like, you can choose how much of Apple's computational photography you want to apply to images.

321
00:26:38,560 --> 00:26:39,680
And we built that into the app.

322
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:44,480
So, you know, I'd say that we're learning some of these features.

323
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:55,840
Like, I want to pretend that I was, I knew all this stuff before I wrote the post, but sometimes I'll get knee deep in a post and then just go down this deep Wikipedia hole like, oh my God, I didn't know this is how you do this thing.

324
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,720
So it's been, it's been just as much fun for me to learn this stuff.

325
00:26:59,200 --> 00:26:59,280
Yeah.

326
00:26:59,520 --> 00:27:10,880
I I feel like I'm sort of virtuous, uh, mutual beneficial thing going on between you and the other camera apps and and Apple.

327
00:27:11,360 --> 00:27:33,600
It's like it seems like it could be seen as a competitive thing, um, but it really is like the things that you guys are trying to solve are definitely different from a design perspective than what the generic photos app that or camera app that comes on literally every person in the world's iPhone uh, has to solve for.

328
00:27:34,160 --> 00:27:42,560
And so it's almost like you guys are a release vi valve for those teams probably to be like they probably want to add a lot of this stuff but can't.

329
00:27:42,800 --> 00:27:56,080
And so by giving API access, it's almost like they get to sort of give those to the iPhone community, but not necessarily through this app that needs to be accessible and packed with a bajillion features.

330
00:27:57,120 --> 00:27:57,200
Yeah.

331
00:27:57,600 --> 00:28:10,240
I mean, I I have nothing but immense admiration and respect for the camera team at Apple because I don't think anybody truly gives them enough credit for how tough that job is.

332
00:28:10,480 --> 00:28:13,840
They have to make a camera that works for every single iPhone user.

333
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:18,240
I'm not sure if I would ever want to be in those shoes.

334
00:28:19,360 --> 00:28:20,240
I like my niche.

335
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:23,200
I like having choosing my user and designing our product.

336
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:28,320
I can sit here and talk about how we love to have an opinionated product, et cetera.

337
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:30,000
That's not a luxury they can afford.

338
00:28:30,320 --> 00:28:31,840
They cannot make an opinionated product.

339
00:28:32,880 --> 00:28:38,160
I'd also say that it's also like by choice that it's by choice.

340
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:41,680
We're also not trying to overlap too much in the first party camera.

341
00:28:42,240 --> 00:29:04,320
I think that if you're looking at building your own app and it is, you know, it's reskinning the services of the main thing or the service, like if you're building, you know, for example, a third-party Twitter client, you know, if you can't answer how your thing is going to be different, well, the bigger company is, you know, Apple has how many billions of dollars in the bank?

342
00:29:04,480 --> 00:29:07,680
They've got like, I'm sure a ton of engineers working on their camera system.

343
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,160
I don't think we could compete with them if we wanted to.

344
00:29:10,400 --> 00:29:14,000
Like, I don't have a PhD in computational photography.

345
00:29:14,400 --> 00:29:23,360
So I think that we're also really smart about focusing on like something that is different and answering the question, okay, how are we different than the first-party camera?

346
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:28,880
And, you know, hoping that there'ss a large enough market that there are people who will want those things we're going to be building.

347
00:29:29,200 --> 00:29:56,320
Yeah, I think one of the things that makes phone photography so delightful and the reason why it's really made it infrequent that I pull out my SLR anymore is instead of having a bunch of different lenses or maybe even bodies that I switch between whenever I'm somewhere to try out different things and experiment, I have like a bunch of different apps with a whole bunch of different features that are all playing with different things.

348
00:29:56,560 --> 00:30:05,120
Like when I go on like a vacation or hike or something, I definitely use HoloLite a bunch, but I also definitely use the default camera app a bunch.

349
00:30:05,440 --> 00:30:09,760
And I definitely use Spectre, or Spectre, no, see, I've already mispronounced it.

