00:33:17,840 --> 00:33:25,280
And then I think after sending all that out, it's kind of a weird feeling because then you're just like, okay, we flipped the switch to make it available for release on the App Store.

388
00:33:26,080 --> 00:33:28,160
We tweeted about it and sent the emails.

389
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:30,400
So now you, I guess we wait.

390
00:33:30,880 --> 00:33:35,360
Yeah, nobody prepares you for how empty feeling a launch is, uh, immediately.

391
00:33:35,600 --> 00:33:36,000
Oh my God.

392
00:33:36,640 --> 00:33:47,600
You expect like, I don't know, fireworks or something, but especially when you're alone in your house or just with one other person in your room or something, it's very, almost anticlimactic.

393
00:33:48,160 --> 00:33:48,960
Yeah, totally.

394
00:33:49,520 --> 00:33:50,000
Well, yeah.

395
00:33:50,480 --> 00:34:01,360
I think also, you know, whenever you do a big launch, you end up with that nice spike of downloads the first day or two, and then you've seen the charts everywhere where everything becomes sort of levels out to what it is.

396
00:34:01,840 --> 00:34:03,200
Like, what's the normal cadence?

397
00:34:03,440 --> 00:34:07,280
And that can also be like, you can fool yourself into being like, okay, this is great.

398
00:34:07,520 --> 00:34:08,640
Nothing is going to ever top this.

399
00:34:08,720 --> 00:34:09,680
It's going to be like this forever.

400
00:34:09,840 --> 00:34:10,640
Like, no, no, no.

401
00:34:10,880 --> 00:34:12,880
No matter how big the launch, you get like one new cycle.

402
00:34:13,440 --> 00:34:16,400
and then it kind of settles down to where you can expect day to day.

403
00:34:17,120 --> 00:34:27,280
And so I think that the other thing that helped with us is that we kept building on the product after, for whatever reason, we just kept building it after the initial buzz and the hype of having launched it.

404
00:34:27,520 --> 00:34:38,320
And I think that even though the launch itself was positive, it was critical for us to stick to it after that initial dopamine rush and keep investing in it month after month.

405
00:34:38,800 --> 00:34:48,320
And we saw it more sustainable to the point that now we're working with someone else, Rebecca, and we have other folks helping us out with the support.

406
00:34:48,960 --> 00:34:52,080
So it did take some work to actually grow it into a mature company.

407
00:34:52,240 --> 00:34:56,480
It wasn't just like, you know, you launch once and great, you're all set and then you never have to worry again.

408
00:34:56,880 --> 00:34:56,960
Yeah.

409
00:34:57,440 --> 00:34:57,600
Yeah.

410
00:34:58,240 --> 00:35:03,920
There's two other things that I don't know if these actually are big marketing things for you, but in my head they seem like they would be.

411
00:35:04,240 --> 00:35:16,560
And one is we've talked about it a couple of times, but those blog posts that both of you guys do, those come out now like it's on the lists with like Austin Man reviews and maybe an MKBHD review or something.

412
00:35:16,800 --> 00:35:24,640
For the thing I'm looking forward to after a phone, any phone or any device release with a camera on it is your like tech.

413
00:35:24,640 --> 00:35:28,160
your technical review of what they've done under the hood.

414
00:35:29,680 --> 00:35:31,360
Has that kind of turned into a marketing channel?

415
00:35:31,600 --> 00:35:35,520
I assume it wasn't intentional at first, but maybe that was all part of the plan all along.

416
00:35:35,920 --> 00:35:36,000
Yeah.

417
00:35:36,640 --> 00:35:46,080
You know, if you were to take a particularly cynical lens and some people on Reddit have this or you know those comments sections, Hacker News, I'm not naming any names, Hacker News.

418
00:35:46,720 --> 00:35:58,000
You know, everything is everything you do, if you even mention something, a project of your own that could have the potential to make you a few dollars in revenue if someone downloads it, is a marketing thing.

419
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:06,800
But I think what really helps with this is is that we don't do it with an attitude of treating it like a marketing thing.

420
00:36:07,040 --> 00:36:08,960
So we do it because we're super passionate about it.

421
00:36:09,120 --> 00:36:14,080
And I've actually been working on the iPhone 13 Pro on for weeks now because it's a really challenging one.

422
00:36:14,320 --> 00:36:16,800
And a lot of people have been asking, like, where is the iPhone post?

423
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:20,480
And if it was a marketing thing, you know, we just push it out the first week.

424
00:36:20,640 --> 00:36:23,680
Right, it would be beneficial to get it out while the news is still hot.

425
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:25,680
Yeah, a strike when the iron's hot, you know.

426
00:36:25,760 --> 00:36:33,040
And that's also what a lot of people like people that are in the YouTube scene because of the algorithm, they're actually kind of their hands are tied in that.

427
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:47,200
And we found that it does work great for getting the word out about our little app, but we also definitely don't go too heavy handed in every other paragraph for like, hey, you should download the app.

428
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:57,040
And there have been times where it also just overlaps, you know, like the things we were talking about just happened to tie into a feature we were launching, but yeah, overall it's, yeah, it's a little bit of both.

429
00:36:57,440 --> 00:37:06,320
We've actually, I think, had times where we have written the full post and then, well, as we're finishing up the draft, we realize, oh yeah, we should mention somewhere we make a camera app.

430
00:37:06,880 --> 00:37:08,480
Like, we should mention somewhere.

431
00:37:08,880 --> 00:37:30,080
So there's like, I'd say that there's a very clear, like, editorial wall, if you will, between, okay, this is a thing that stands on its own and to Sebastian's point, like, you know, we will, we'll, the fact that our articles help us sell the app isn't a coincidence because we're building the app around the new hardware, right?

432
00:37:30,400 --> 00:37:38,320
So as an example with a macro feature, there were like tea leaves that you could have read earlier in the year that there's probably going to be some kind of macro we kind of thing coming out in fall.

433
00:37:38,880 --> 00:37:44,720
And so I was already looking around summer time as to how a macro feature would look like when we launch it.

434
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:48,800
And then naturally, when the feature comes out, of course, we'd write an article about macro.

435
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:52,800
So, you know, so it all it's usually a happy accent.

436
00:37:53,120 --> 00:38:00,480
And yeah, I just I wish there was some kind of class we could give on this, but no, no, we just write articles we want to read.

437
00:38:00,880 --> 00:38:04,080
Well, and they're definitely more readable because of that, right?

438
00:38:04,320 --> 00:38:18,240
Like there's plenty of, you know, top ten tips for XYZ that are clearly just kind of generic fluff that are generated to get you on a company's blog so that hopefully you see their other stuff.

439
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:19,520
And these are definitely not that.

440
00:38:20,400 --> 00:38:27,440
I can't tell you how many people I'vem annoying and I'm always like pitching it to people.

441
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:39,840
And usually what I'll say is, you know those crazy in-depth reviews, which most of my friends know because again, I send them those, but they know the review as a separate entity than this product.

442
00:38:40,160 --> 00:38:41,920
And they're like, Oh yeah, it's those people.

443
00:38:42,240 --> 00:38:51,040
And it lends credibility to the app itself whenever it's like, Yeah, these guys know what they're talking about as far as what's happening under the hood here too.

444
00:38:51,360 --> 00:38:52,640
Oh, that's awesome to hear.

445
00:38:52,960 --> 00:38:53,760
That's really, really cool.

446
00:38:54,160 --> 00:38:58,000
I something you just said, Ben, I'm kind of interested to pull in a little bit.

447
00:38:58,240 --> 00:39:02,800
So you mentioned like you were reading the tea leav leaves a little bit about the macro mode.

448
00:39:03,280 --> 00:39:11,040
Does that mean you were kind of preparing for that coming and figuring out designs and stuff in advance?

449
00:39:11,360 --> 00:39:14,800
And does that help with how quickly you guys got that feature out?

450
00:39:15,280 --> 00:39:24,880
Well, so going back to the iPhone X, like there were already leaks like six months before the iPhone X launched via, you know, nine to five Mac.

451
00:39:25,600 --> 00:39:33,200
So I think in that case we were already having the gears moving in our heads around what a bezel based iPhone design would look like.

452
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:39,920
And Sebastian knocked it out of the park with coming up with a histogram that sits inside of the ears of the device, right?

453
00:39:40,960 --> 00:39:48,480
So in the case of macro, there were APIs that are around figuring out the minimum focus distance of the device.

454
00:39:49,200 --> 00:39:53,120
So there's that clue and it's like, okay, why would you launch this this year?

455
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:54,640
Why this year would you come up with that?

456
00:39:54,800 --> 00:39:57,120
That's a really weird thing to just decide one day to launch.

457
00:39:57,600 --> 00:40:04,160
And then combine with, okay, clearly Apple is kind of trying to come up with reasons to upgrade your device.

458
00:40:04,320 --> 00:40:06,080
So they're every year investing in new hardware.

459
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:09,360
It would make sense that the next frontier is to do something macro related.

460
00:40:09,360 --> 00:40:10,560
They already have a telephoto.

461
00:40:10,720 --> 00:40:11,600
They have an ultra wide.

462
00:40:11,840 --> 00:40:13,680
It would be some kind of macro, right?

463
00:40:14,080 --> 00:40:27,920
So we had conversations about it and I started, you know, doing deeper research around training the neural network, uh, where if we wanted to do an upscale or, um, you know, an ML based, uh, super resolution, then how would you accomplish that?

464
00:40:28,320 --> 00:40:34,480
But I don't think that we really fully committed, we didn't invest in it until they announced, oh, it's got a macro feature.

465
00:40:34,560 --> 00:40:36,000
And then it was like hitting the ground running.

466
00:40:36,320 --> 00:40:42,080
But it's like, um, I don't know what's the saying, like, if I had to cut down a cherry tree, I would spend eight hours sharpening the ash.

467
00:40:42,160 --> 00:40:42,240
Yeah.

468
00:40:43,120 --> 00:40:43,680
Like, yeah.

469
00:40:44,000 --> 00:40:44,720
So, yeah.

470
00:40:44,960 --> 00:40:48,800
So you never know till Apple launches something, but we try to prep, I think, uh, in advance.

471
00:40:49,200 --> 00:40:50,000
based on rumors.

472
00:40:50,800 --> 00:40:54,560
Do you find that it's beneficial to get that out quickly?

473
00:40:55,120 --> 00:41:10,800
Do you put pressure on yourself to be early, maybe not the first person to do something in this space, but to get there early, or is it like you'd much rather just spend a lot of time, all the time you need, or maybe even finish some other work before you get on that?

474
00:41:11,680 --> 00:41:20,240
I think that there's a lot to be said for, you know, striking while the iron's hot, but I think also that comes with recognizing what you can ship in your capacity at the time.

475
00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:24,000
Like there's a lot of other stuff I'd love to build in in the app.

476
00:41:24,240 --> 00:41:30,320
app, but that I knew you couldn't do in the short time frame between them announcing it and when they're launching it.

477
00:41:30,800 --> 00:41:40,320
So yeah, I mean, I'd say that the hardest time of the year is when the new hardware drops, and that's pretty much the only time of the year when I'm okay with working with a weekend.

478
00:41:41,520 --> 00:41:51,680
And I think we also try to set boundaries and we joke about locking people out of the repo during the holidays so that you don't get you don't work when you're supposed to take vacation time.

479
00:41:51,840 --> 00:41:52,560
Quote unquote.

480
00:41:52,720 --> 00:41:55,920
So we're trying to Yeah.

481
00:41:57,360 --> 00:42:41,920
So, you know, I think that inevitably at a company in the same thing if you have a server like you you want to set a right work life balance but look sometimes a server is going to go down and you have to you know go be on call on a weekend but yeah I think the only time of year when we find that really acceptable is when there's new hardware dropping and if we're going to burn the midnight oil that's typically the time we do it yeah and I I guess I should phrase it a little differently I guess what I mean is um you seem to jump on new hardware things really quickly um whether it's literal like new lenses or stuff or just a new software feature that comes out uh you you seem to be very fast at jumping on that I assume you also have a product roadmap that's totally separated from Apple's hardware release schedule and everything.

482
00:42:42,480 --> 00:42:59,680
So are you, in terms of how you're prioritizing things, do you sort of set yourself up so that at any point you can drop whatever it is you're currently working on and let that sit on the side while you do this strike while the irons hot type feature?

483
00:43:00,880 --> 00:43:02,720
Is that like an intentional thing that you're doing?

484
00:43:03,120 --> 00:43:07,120
Yeah, I mean, it also sucks that it means our roadmap pauses.

485
00:43:07,520 --> 00:43:07,680
Right.

486
00:43:07,920 --> 00:43:10,240
Apple throws a curve ball in fall.

487
00:43:10,640 --> 00:43:20,080
And I mean, that's one of the nice things of what we're trying to go for and the reason that we now have Rebecca as part of our team is it being able to walk and chew gum at the same time.

488
00:43:20,880 --> 00:43:25,760
But it, you know, you have to be careful where you're not constantly distracted by the new and shiny, right?

489
00:43:26,560 --> 00:43:29,120
And there are material benefits of getting something out sooner.

490
00:43:29,360 --> 00:43:32,720
You know, if you want to be featured in the app store, just put yourself in Apple shoes.

491
00:43:32,960 --> 00:43:34,000
They came out with new hardware.

492
00:43:34,320 --> 00:43:37,520
They're going to want to feature apps that feature that new hardware, right?

493
00:43:38,240 --> 00:43:39,760
But you also need to choose the right battles.

494
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:42,560
Like we didn't build iMessage extensions.

495
00:43:43,280 --> 00:43:52,960
We, you know, you have to choose what, you know, you can't just build a feature for the sake of trying to get PR because it's going to read as hollow and it's going to be a distraction.

496
00:43:53,280 --> 00:44:00,800
So we pick the right battles and we try to prioritize what's going to help us, especially with the holidays, people getting new phones.

497
00:44:01,360 --> 00:44:05,760
But yeah, yeah, I'd say that it is important to ship features sooner rather than later.

498
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:08,640
Yeah, and I mean, talk about roadmap.

499
00:44:08,880 --> 00:44:19,760
I mean, we are also like in the non envyable position that we know that typically the new iPhone coming out is going to break the app in some sort of way.

500
00:44:20,000 --> 00:44:20,160
Yeah.

501
00:44:20,400 --> 00:44:24,160
Because there's like the new camera or this or like a completely underlying system doesn't work.

502
00:44:24,640 --> 00:44:30,720
Like when the iPhone XS started, before the iPhone XS, there were all these underlying changes in the depth API and stuff like that.

503
00:44:30,880 --> 00:44:34,960
And we realized like stuff was going to happen in the betas before the iPhone came out.

504
00:44:35,120 --> 00:44:37,280
And so we were already kind of prepared for things being busted.

505
00:44:37,520 --> 00:44:50,800
But I think most of the people listening to this podcast are fortunately, you know, would be quite patient and understanding of that happening, like because they know that we don't get invited down to Cupertino despite our proximity to come sit with Tim Cook in a room.

506
00:44:51,120 --> 00:44:53,040
And he says, hey, these are new iPhones.

507
00:44:53,520 --> 00:44:57,840
How can we make sure that your app isn't horrendously broken when these come out?

508
00:44:58,560 --> 00:44:59,680
But we have to rush to the store.

509
00:44:59,920 --> 00:45:04,000
Most of our users are not that understanding with it.

510
00:45:04,160 --> 00:45:09,280
So we rush to the store as soon as possible to get the new phones and make sure that everything we get an update that fixes things.

511
00:45:09,600 --> 00:45:12,880
But then we also can't help but get super excited about what's new and try to support that.

512
00:45:13,520 --> 00:45:23,200
Because honestly at that point often when it comes to like camera features, you know, Apple adds a lens or Apple adds a particular fundamental aspect of the camera experience.

513
00:45:24,560 --> 00:45:27,920
It's almost like fixing an egregious bug if you don't support it.

514
00:45:28,000 --> 00:45:30,160
Even though for us it could be quite a difficult feature to build.

515
00:45:30,640 --> 00:45:36,720
If there is a new thing that the camera does, if you don't do it as a camera app, you know, you don't stack up very well.

516
00:45:37,440 --> 00:45:40,240
So for us, sometimes we kind of bake that into the road map.

517
00:45:40,560 --> 00:45:46,160
As new iPhone season, we know that we're going to have our heads down with reactive rather than planned changes.

518
00:45:46,960 --> 00:45:50,560
And we do try to read the tea leaves a little bit and try to figure out what's going to happen.

519
00:45:51,360 --> 00:45:53,600
And hopefully that aligns with like our strategy.

520
00:45:53,840 --> 00:45:56,240
But we're also, of course, we're a camera app for an iPhone.

521
00:45:56,320 --> 00:46:00,080
So we have to work with the cameras and the features that the iPhone is going to have.

522
00:46:00,880 --> 00:46:00,960
Yeah.

523
00:46:01,280 --> 00:46:06,240
That makes a lot of sense, especially for a premium camera app.

524
00:46:06,720 --> 00:46:12,800
It probably has a higher percentage of users that are also on the latest premium camera.

525
00:46:12,800 --> 00:46:16,800
Premium iPhone and those users are expecting it to work together.

526
00:46:17,520 --> 00:46:25,360
So from a business sense, it probably actually also makes sense as well as a general shiny excitement sense from your perspective.

527
00:46:26,720 --> 00:46:27,120
Oh yeah.

528
00:46:27,200 --> 00:46:37,120
And I think on the flip side, just like dealing with surprise bugs that so like big apps like Instagram don't use these types of camera APIs that we're using.

529
00:46:37,360 --> 00:46:41,200
Like they don't even load raws, which is kind of funny for a photo app, right?

530
00:46:42,400 --> 00:46:47,680
But as a result, we end up touching a bunch of the edge cases that very few apps touch.

531
00:46:48,320 --> 00:46:57,440
And so we need to be filing more radars and giving a heads up of, hey, it turns out if you combine this file format with this pixel format, this thing doesn't work.

532
00:46:58,080 --> 00:47:08,880
And so a lot of it is just like trying to find ways of working around with edge cases and bugs that can crop up because we're kind of pushing a few boundaries on the tech side.

533
00:47:09,200 --> 00:47:12,640
That can be disheartening because we want to be shipping new features all the time.

534
00:47:13,280 --> 00:47:26,240
But as we keep pushing the boundaries or pushing like the latest APIs, a lot of it is like dealing with technical surprises as opposed to, you know, early on when we launched Hailil Light, it was just like ship, ship, ship.

535
00:47:26,400 --> 00:47:28,080
And so trying to find a balance there, you know?

536
00:47:28,560 --> 00:47:30,560
Yeah, that definitely makes sense.

537
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:37,920
And I imagine, like you said earlier, having a third person with Rebecca on now is making a big difference.

538
00:47:38,160 --> 00:47:39,200
And I want to get to that.

539
00:47:39,440 --> 00:47:48,160
But leading to that moment, I'm going to assume required, and you can correct me if I'm wrong, a business model change.

540
00:47:48,640 --> 00:47:58,880
And you guys went through a pretty major business model change about a year ago at this point with HoloLife 2 and switching from a single paid app front app to a subscription app.

