00:50:33,600 --> 00:50:35,120
Yeah, it's a really good question.

570
00:50:35,520 --> 00:50:45,280
It is actually it is a response to the most frequently requested feature, which stemmed from people fundamentally misunderstanding the iPhone camera.

571
00:50:45,440 --> 00:50:55,920
So just a short like sort of segue from that, the most requested feature we got because we have a manual exposure mode was people saying, why can't I expose for more than a third of a second?

572
00:50:56,320 --> 00:50:58,000
This was back then when that was the maximum.

573
00:50:58,240 --> 00:50:59,680
And we said, well, that's just your iPhone.

574
00:50:59,840 --> 00:51:02,560
That's just the iPhone camera doesn't expose longer than that.

575
00:51:02,880 --> 00:51:06,080
And then people said, well, but my camera does that.

576
00:51:06,320 --> 00:51:07,360
So why can't you do it?

577
00:51:07,520 --> 00:51:08,880
And we're like, well, you can't.

578
00:51:09,200 --> 00:51:10,080
You'd have to fake it.

579
00:51:10,240 --> 00:51:11,520
And then people are like, I don't care.

580
00:51:12,080 --> 00:51:16,320
And well, we didn't really say, we didn't have a dialogue like that, but that was kind of the general gist of it.

581
00:51:16,480 --> 00:51:24,160
And then we thought, okay, well, we don't think this really fits in the app, but we could make a really cool long exposure thing.

582
00:51:25,040 --> 00:51:34,960
And then Ben and I got together and we ended up like, wow, this could be really magical because in addition to that, like this is going to be, again, kind of looking at why people are asking for this.

583
00:51:35,600 --> 00:51:36,560
You could get really cool stuff.

584
00:51:37,120 --> 00:51:44,640
that is way, way more usable than even using your camera, because we could use all the fancy dudads in your phone to automatically stabilize the shot.

585
00:51:44,720 --> 00:51:46,000
You don't need a tripod anymore.

586
00:51:46,160 --> 00:51:47,920
You could do handheld nine second exposures.

587
00:51:48,000 --> 00:51:49,360
And we're like, okay, this is really cool.

588
00:51:49,440 --> 00:51:50,720
Let's break it into a separate product.

589
00:51:51,200 --> 00:51:56,000
So in that sense, yeah, it wasn't, it was an experiment, but not so much to keep Halight alive.

590
00:51:57,920 --> 00:52:07,120
We definitely realized that with how much effort we had to put into that and how much effort it is to keep the app current, that it wouldn't be a very scalable solution to just keep making new apps.

591
00:52:07,360 --> 00:52:12,880
And we very much realized that if we wanted to make like Halight V two, Old Hill the New King, the Old King is dead..

592
00:52:13,440 --> 00:52:14,640
Everybody go buy this new app.

593
00:52:14,960 --> 00:52:16,640
How hard that would be for the existing user base.

594
00:52:17,120 --> 00:52:17,280
Right.

595
00:52:17,600 --> 00:52:19,680
And how difficult it would be to try to maintain both.

596
00:52:19,920 --> 00:52:25,520
And yeah, the longer we looked at that, we just thought, okay, this is not going to be a very sustainable.

597
00:52:26,480 --> 00:52:28,080
Sustainability was really the thing we had in mind.

598
00:52:28,160 --> 00:52:29,200
We want to keep HALED going.

599
00:52:29,520 --> 00:52:40,240
Spectre, we were really happy with the way it was and we didn't think it would need a ton of ongoing maintenance because we didn't want to keep building it out as the de facto camera app that is a kind of a general purpose thing.

600
00:52:41,520 --> 00:52:43,360
Which means you don't have to support as many new things.

601
00:52:43,760 --> 00:52:49,200
And it's that, yeah, we were really running into the limits of sustainability there.

602
00:52:49,600 --> 00:52:52,560
That's why we decided to think about changing the business model.

603
00:52:54,560 --> 00:53:05,840
It was an interesting experiment with Spectre, but as far as demand from users around updates, I would say that there's way more momentum around, hey, could you do this with Halide?

604
00:53:06,400 --> 00:53:08,640
Apple every year keeps upgrading its cameras.

605
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:18,480
All of the momentum, external momentum is around Halide, and users are pretty happy right now with Spectre as it is right now where it is.

606
00:53:19,440 --> 00:53:22,160
But yeah, it's just like we're mostly just listening to users.

607
00:53:22,640 --> 00:53:32,880
I don't know if what we'd want to invest in Spectre versus like right now, like all the momentum's around Halide.

608
00:53:33,200 --> 00:53:34,720
Yeah, yeah, that definitely makes sense.

609
00:53:34,960 --> 00:53:42,480
I was just curious if, like you had mentioned, building new apps would be one way to have a sustainable future potentially.

610
00:53:42,800 --> 00:53:51,120
And what my brain immediately went to is essentially like breaking off features that you would add to Holide into separate apps.

611
00:53:52,000 --> 00:53:53,520
And Spectre would sort of look like that.

612
00:53:53,760 --> 00:53:56,720
But selfishly, I'm glad you went with what you went with.

613
00:53:57,520 --> 00:54:03,440
Because I do think that it fits a lot better with at least what I enjoy out of Hollide.

614
00:54:04,000 --> 00:54:10,320
But so you had mentioned that Rebecca, you had already brought her on before you switched over.

615
00:54:11,680 --> 00:54:11,760
Yeah.

616
00:54:12,800 --> 00:54:16,960
Ben, correct me if I'm wrong, but I think Mark II launched October.

617
00:54:18,400 --> 00:54:20,240
October 23, I think.

618
00:54:20,800 --> 00:54:20,880
Yeah.

619
00:54:21,440 --> 00:54:22,080
2020.

620
00:54:22,320 --> 00:54:22,400
Yeah.

621
00:54:23,360 --> 00:54:27,680
So she already, like, In advance, we told her this is what we're looking at.

622
00:54:28,240 --> 00:54:28,960
This is Mark II.

623
00:54:29,280 --> 00:54:31,600
This is our plan around how we're going to be transitioning.

624
00:54:32,000 --> 00:54:34,000
Or this is we're planning to transition the business model.

625
00:54:34,480 --> 00:54:39,680
You know, this is kind of like where we're looking at the company is the big, big picture to like what we'd love to be doing in the future.

626
00:54:40,240 --> 00:54:42,960
So it's not like she joined one day and we kept everything secret.

627
00:54:43,280 --> 00:54:43,680
Right, right.

628
00:54:44,480 --> 00:54:47,440
She joined in May of that year.

629
00:54:47,920 --> 00:54:54,960
And so she was invaluable in helping us work out a lot of the inappropriate purchase kinks and all those surprises there.

630
00:54:55,200 --> 00:54:59,440
So it was great having, I don't think we could have shipped in October of that year without her help.

631
00:54:59,920 --> 00:55:03,760
That's what I was going to ask is what was the motivation for bringing on a new person?

632
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:12,000
Because that's, I feel like that's this huge step, the very first person, because it's sort of, it changes the whole dynamic, right?

633
00:55:13,120 --> 00:55:19,920
And it would be scary because now you have like a salary and a person that's sort of dependent on the app being successful going forward.

634
00:55:20,720 --> 00:55:23,840
What made you choose to take that leap in the first place?

635
00:55:24,240 --> 00:55:30,800
So I think after we won iPhone App of the Year for Spectre, we had this sort of existential question, what do we want to do now?

636
00:55:31,200 --> 00:55:32,880
Like we could just keep doing these two apps.

637
00:55:33,520 --> 00:55:34,480
And that's great.

638
00:55:35,120 --> 00:55:39,920
But I think that both Sebastian and I, you know, we love continuing to build new things.

639
00:55:40,800 --> 00:55:44,560
And just the two of us, we've reached a bottleneck in hours of the day.

640
00:55:45,520 --> 00:55:49,680
You know, I would love to, I mean, spoiler, one day we'll have 1100 apps, no.

641
00:55:50,560 --> 00:55:53,520
I'd love to, it may not even look, it may not even be another app.

642
00:55:53,760 --> 00:55:55,840
It may be features in Halide that we want to build.

643
00:55:56,560 --> 00:56:09,280
And the reality is you can run a successful business with a small team of two people, but then, you know, if you want to be tackling more difficult problems, inevitably you need to expand or you need to stop sleeping.

644
00:56:10,800 --> 00:56:15,200
Give up all your loved ones, never see them again and just, you know, work 24/7.

645
00:56:15,360 --> 00:56:20,400
So I think it's a step towards having a sustainable way to continue to build more stuff.

646
00:56:20,880 --> 00:56:26,000
And how did you approach, not to go too deep in the nitty gritty, but how did you approach it looking for a new person?

647
00:56:26,240 --> 00:56:33,840
Was it just like you, you already knew Rebecca or did you like did a kind of extensive search for for who to bring on?

648
00:56:34,400 --> 00:56:37,200
I can only speak for myself, but Rebecca was literally our dream person.

649
00:56:37,440 --> 00:56:48,480
Like it was, it was the thing where we, we very early on started to think like, well, if we could hire like, it would be bizarre idea, but like, if we would ever be able to hire anyone we would, you know, want to bring on part of the team.

650
00:56:48,960 --> 00:56:51,040
And I think for both of us, that was sister Rebecca.

651
00:56:51,120 --> 00:56:54,240
It's like the first person we thought of and we didn't want to settle for anything less.

652
00:56:55,520 --> 00:56:55,600
Yeah.

653
00:56:56,320 --> 00:57:01,040
My question is, if she's half as funny in real life as she is on Twitter, how do you get any work done??

654
00:57:02,160 --> 00:57:02,640
We don't.

655
00:57:04,880 --> 00:57:06,320
Yeah, we do a lot of riffing.

656
00:57:07,920 --> 00:57:11,440
I think we have two meetings a week officially at the beginning and the end of the week.

657
00:57:11,760 --> 00:57:17,360
And so yeah, we're probably the funniest small app development team there.

658
00:57:17,600 --> 00:57:22,400
So, oh man, that's awesome.

659
00:57:22,640 --> 00:57:35,280
So with the switch over to not sorry, not the switch to subscription, with the addition of a new subscription model on top of the paid upfront model or onetime payment model, you also added a free tier.

660
00:57:35,360 --> 00:57:37,520
So I guess there's a whole lot going on on here.

661
00:57:39,040 --> 00:57:39,920
How was that received?

662
00:57:40,480 --> 00:57:42,000
Did it match kind of your expectations?

663
00:57:42,800 --> 00:57:44,080
Well, I do have to clarify.

664
00:57:44,720 --> 00:57:45,200
We have a trial.

665
00:57:45,360 --> 00:57:47,280
So like with the thing, you can try it for two weeks.

666
00:57:47,360 --> 00:57:48,240
We don't have a free tier.

667
00:57:48,400 --> 00:57:49,360
We're not freemium.

668
00:57:49,520 --> 00:57:49,840
Oh, okay.

669
00:57:50,000 --> 00:57:51,120
I'm glad you clarified that.

670
00:57:51,360 --> 00:57:54,160
Just check the app store, just check those app store reviews though.

671
00:57:54,400 --> 00:57:54,480
Yeah.

672
00:57:54,800 --> 00:57:56,400
They also get confused.

673
00:57:57,120 --> 00:57:57,200
Yeah.

674
00:57:57,840 --> 00:57:58,480
That's a tough thing.

675
00:57:58,640 --> 00:58:02,240
So to say in advance, like this is a thing the app store, I think, we think should really fix.

676
00:58:02,720 --> 00:58:09,680
If you do something like this, you have to put a paywall in your app, and so people can choose their payment option, which requires you to make your app free to download.

677
00:58:10,160 --> 00:58:16,320
So some parts of the app store will still say your app is free., which is really highly problematic because our app is not free.

678
00:58:16,480 --> 00:58:19,600
We don't want anyone to think that it's free and some people very much complain.

679
00:58:19,760 --> 00:58:26,160
We have a torrent of one star reviews or people that say, like, this is a bait and switch scamy developer or whatever.

680
00:58:26,720 --> 00:58:30,960
It's really honestly very annoying and we wish we could mark a very clear flag on it.

681
00:58:32,160 --> 00:58:34,880
We put it at the top of the description, like, this is not a free app.

682
00:58:35,520 --> 00:58:37,360
I don't know if there's anything we can do.

683
00:58:38,080 --> 00:58:39,840
You don't get the same response from a Netflix.

684
00:58:40,240 --> 00:58:42,640
You don't download Netflix, like, wait, I thought this was free, right?

685
00:58:43,120 --> 00:58:47,680
So we're very limited in the affordances the App Store gives us for communicating it.

686
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:48,000
Yeah.

687
00:58:48,160 --> 00:58:52,000
Yeah, and I think a good story about the reception is actually kind of the launch day.

688
00:58:52,400 --> 00:58:54,480
We had a bit of a launch day snag.

689
00:58:55,120 --> 00:58:56,320
Ren, you want to talk about it?

690
00:58:57,840 --> 00:59:08,480
So, with inside, if you miss in the docs, there's like basically a way that you're supposed to check app store receipts before you launch the app in the sandbox environment versus production.

691
00:59:09,280 --> 00:59:13,920
And both me and Rebecca had missed the little disclaimer around it.

692
00:59:14,320 --> 00:59:22,800
And the long and the short of it is we were supposed to automatically grandfather in everyone who had bought halide before you got mark two for free.

693
00:59:24,160 --> 00:59:34,720
For like when we launched, it turned out just because of the way the receipts were structured, they were not getting the free upgrade and everyone assumed that we were those evil developers who were taking away something they already bought.

694
00:59:35,680 --> 00:59:37,520
It just leans right into the narrative.

695
00:59:39,040 --> 00:59:39,840
Yeah, exactly.

696
00:59:40,160 --> 00:59:41,600
And then, you know, yeah.

697
00:59:42,640 --> 00:59:53,680
So it was a very tense fifteen minutes writing a patch that we couldn't test in advance, submitting it to Apple and then doing everything we could to ping Apple.

698
00:59:54,400 --> 00:59:56,480
Hey, hey, could you expedit this as quickly as possible?

699
00:59:56,720 --> 00:59:57,280
Please.

700
00:59:57,680 --> 00:59:58,640
To Apple's credit.

701
00:59:58,960 --> 00:59:59,040
Yeah.

702
01:00:00,320 --> 01:00:02,080
Within an hour we had the patch out there.

703
01:00:02,400 --> 01:00:09,200
And I think before the press push, we had sent out press kits and reached out to all our contacts.

704
01:00:09,840 --> 01:00:13,600
But even just that one hour was like a torrent of one star reviews.

705
01:00:14,080 --> 01:00:18,000
And eventually, we've been messaging to people about, no, this was a mistake.

706
01:00:18,160 --> 01:00:19,120
And then everyone understood.

707
01:00:19,360 --> 01:00:21,200
But yeah, that first hour was a nightmare.

708
01:00:21,440 --> 01:00:23,520
Like the last eighteen months had been for naught.

709
01:00:23,680 --> 01:00:25,040
We were giving this away for no reason.

710
01:00:25,920 --> 01:00:26,880
Yeah, it was pretty bad.

711
01:00:27,440 --> 01:00:27,920
Oh man.

712
01:00:28,640 --> 01:00:37,680
Yeah, I feel like it seems like every one of these subscription changes in business models always seems to have issues like this.

713
01:00:38,160 --> 01:00:44,480
And I guess it's it's related to the fact that you can't really test it for real until it's actually live out there.

714
01:00:45,120 --> 01:00:45,200
Yeah.

715
01:00:45,920 --> 01:00:46,000
Yeah.

716
01:00:46,560 --> 01:00:48,880
And it's really hard to communicate.

717
01:00:50,080 --> 01:00:57,440
I mean, there is a big discrepancy between understanding the perspective of a developer and the perspective of a user, right?

718
01:00:58,320 --> 01:01:01,520
And it's really hard to exchange money for something that's not tangible.

719
01:01:01,680 --> 01:01:04,720
It's just it's psychologically it's very difficult for us as humans.

720
01:01:04,800 --> 01:01:05,840
And I totally understand that.

721
01:01:06,480 --> 01:01:08,160
that creates a lot of resistance.

722
01:01:08,400 --> 01:01:15,040
So that in addition to the facelessness of downloading an app from the App Store, you don't see that there's people behind it that make it.

723
01:01:15,200 --> 01:01:16,640
You don't see that it's people that are just doing their...

724
01:01:16,640 --> 01:01:18,800
We're not just people that are just doing their passion, just trying to get by doing that.

725
01:01:19,120 --> 01:01:20,240
We're not looking to become rich.

726
01:01:20,800 --> 01:01:26,080
We're not in some sort of field just trying to, like, chase after millions of dollars.

727
01:01:26,320 --> 01:01:28,720
We're just trying to do it, like sustain ourselves doing what we love.

728
01:01:28,960 --> 01:01:32,000
And that disconnect, I think, sometimes is just difficult to overcome.

729
01:01:32,160 --> 01:01:33,920
But I can't help but sympathize with people too.

730
01:01:34,080 --> 01:01:37,600
Like, I totally understand, like, we did this in a really uncertain time.

731
01:01:38,000 --> 01:01:49,360
You know, there was a pandemic that started happening and we realized that was difficult and how privileged we are to have a group of people that support us with a subscription or with a one time purchase.

732
01:01:50,160 --> 01:01:53,360
And I think just to be really sensitive navigating that is actually really the key.

733
01:01:53,520 --> 01:01:54,400
You know, like, try to.

734
01:01:55,200 --> 01:01:58,000
go in and have discussions with your community and with people.

735
01:01:58,160 --> 01:02:05,520
Like, I try to be active in Reddit and on Twitter and stuff to talk to people and be like, hey, this is just how we're doing things and this is why we're making this move.

736
01:02:05,600 --> 01:02:06,960
And like, I'm just a human too.

737
01:02:07,360 --> 01:02:10,480
And if I make mistakes in that, like, that's a great way to find out about that.

738
01:02:10,640 --> 01:02:19,280
Like, we can't make these decisions in a vacuum, you know, they, we are just as dependent on the people using our app as they are on us developing it.

739
01:02:19,440 --> 01:02:23,280
So it's good to like stay, stay, have a healthy perspective in that.

740
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:32,320
Yeah, it does seem like probably fueled by the fact that there are often little bugs that also cause a lot of stress.

741
01:02:32,800 --> 01:02:50,640
But it does seem like there can be on those launch days, especially a, what's the word, combative, uh, maybe response or defensive response, uh, that developers can have in that moment of intense stress where it feels like the whole world is angry at you all at once.

742
01:02:51,440 --> 01:02:52,000
It is really hard.

743
01:02:52,160 --> 01:02:56,800
People start calling you names really quickly and, and, and, you know, you're, you're safe behind your keyboard and you say things.

744
01:02:56,880 --> 01:03:03,920
And, and, and, honestly, like this is also human psychology, that you can get a hundred really nice comments, but that, that single one that's really mean will really, really hurt.

745
01:03:04,000 --> 01:03:05,680
And it'll really, really get you down.

746
01:03:06,160 --> 01:03:08,800
And, um, it's really important to keep perspective in that.

747
01:03:09,040 --> 01:03:18,400
And I, honestly, always, even if if I get a reply that it seems like it's really in bad faith, try to engage with people and say, like, hey, I'm just trying to understand why you feel this way.

748
01:03:18,560 --> 01:03:19,920
And I'm just a human too.

749
01:03:22,400 --> 01:03:25,600
Is this a valid, is this just a troll or is this like a valid concern?

750
01:03:25,840 --> 01:03:28,400
Because I just don't want people to end up with that kind of feeling.

751
01:03:28,480 --> 01:03:31,680
And I feel terrible for the people that are in the reviews saying, like, this is a scam.

752
01:03:32,000 --> 01:03:32,720
Because yeah.

753
01:03:33,040 --> 01:03:34,880
Honestly, it's neither of our faults.

754
01:03:35,120 --> 01:03:38,320
They went in saying they saw a thing that says free app and it looked really cool.

755
01:03:38,480 --> 01:03:39,920
And they downloaded it and it's not.

756
01:03:40,160 --> 01:03:41,520
And we can't fix that either.

757
01:03:41,680 --> 01:03:43,520
And so that just ends up in a little bit of frustration.

758
01:03:43,840 --> 01:03:43,920
Yeah.

759
01:03:44,480 --> 01:03:54,320
That piece in particular is, well, since I've switched over to iOS that has always been a source of frustration is that it's it's really unclear.

760
01:03:55,760 --> 01:04:01,520
If it's a paid up front app, it's very clear what your relationship with this entity is going to be.

761
01:04:02,080 --> 01:04:06,640
But if it's not paid up front, it's just get or the word free at one point.

762
01:04:06,960 --> 01:04:07,920
And like, right.

763
01:04:08,480 --> 01:04:15,360
I've since learned, you know, you can go in and look at the little details and see the list of subscriptions or in-app purchases available.

764
01:04:15,440 --> 01:04:19,040
But that's one, not very descriptive, and two, really hidden.

765
01:04:19,200 --> 01:04:20,720
And I wouldn't expect anyone to see that.

766
01:04:21,040 --> 01:04:30,000
And yeah, I really wish that they, they meaning Apple, would take a pass at that to make that less user hostile.

767
01:04:30,080 --> 01:04:37,600
I don't know if hostile is the right word, but hidden because yeah, it seems like it's just setting people up to be to feel like they were jipped.

768
01:04:38,160 --> 01:04:38,240
Yeah.

769
01:04:38,640 --> 01:04:44,160
And, and, of course, to play Devil's Advocate, I'm sure that it also affects a lot of their biggest customers.

770
01:04:44,320 --> 01:04:48,240
I mean, if you look at the freemium games, you know, people download that because they're free.

771
01:04:48,320 --> 01:04:52,400
And to be fair, they are usually free to play, but then there's like ads or other things.

772
01:04:52,560 --> 01:04:53,760
So yeah, I don't know.

773
01:04:53,920 --> 01:05:00,560
It's also kind of a hard problem to solve, but just the way it is for us right now, it definitely had some had some problems at times.

774
01:05:00,960 --> 01:05:01,040
Yeah.

775
01:05:02,720 --> 01:05:08,160
So we're getting, we're getting pretty close on time on time here, so I want to make sure I get to the last question I ask and I can ask you both.

776
01:05:08,880 --> 01:05:14,240
So, what is a person or people out there that have inspired you that you recommend other people to check out?

777
01:05:15,040 --> 01:05:19,760
I was going to say Summer, I probably butcher her last name, Summer Panaj.

778
01:05:20,800 --> 01:05:27,040
She works at Apple in accessibility things, and I find accessibility things to be astonishingly cool.

779
01:05:27,840 --> 01:05:29,600
There's always more work we need to do there.

780
01:05:29,760 --> 01:05:39,200
We, before she joined Apple, we had her do an audit of our app, and I just really admire the work she does at Apple and accessibility work in general.

781
01:05:39,360 --> 01:05:42,800
It's just an incredibly cool, cool thing and empowering thing to do.

782
01:05:44,800 --> 01:06:00,400
That goes along with the other friends of mine and the design teams at Apple that work on creating the guidelines and doing the work to put out design resources for us to let people like me do my job much more easily.

783
01:06:01,520 --> 01:06:12,160
It's such an unsung kind of hero, the person that goes in and documents all this work they're doing there because they have to move so fast with having to release things, new things every year.

784
01:06:13,680 --> 01:06:17,200
It really makes a huge difference for us and I'm really, really grateful for them.

785
01:06:17,920 --> 01:06:20,640
They've had some amazing people join there recently relatively.

786
01:06:20,640 --> 01:06:26,400
Relatively recently, like Linda Dong and Sir Anthony Caldwell, and they're they're all just fantastic.

787
01:06:26,720 --> 01:06:28,560
And so I really want to give them a shout out.

788
01:06:29,680 --> 01:06:33,920
And of course, those were all people from our community that Apple recently snagged up.

789
01:06:34,160 --> 01:06:34,240
Yeah.

790
01:06:34,480 --> 01:06:36,640
I don't want to be like, Oh, I'm all pro Apple here.

791
01:06:36,800 --> 01:06:41,440
These are all people I already admired before they got absorbed into the mothership.

792
01:06:41,760 --> 01:06:44,080
But those are people definitely come to mind.

793
01:06:44,320 --> 01:06:45,760
Absolutely, absolutely love their work.

794
01:06:46,000 --> 01:06:53,280
I like that though, because I do think I feel like Apple, I don't know, corporate, I don't know what you'd say.

795
01:06:53,520 --> 01:06:55,920
They've gotten better at letting people be a little bit more public.

796
01:06:56,640 --> 01:06:58,640
A lot of times when something comes out, we actually get to.

797
01:06:59,520 --> 01:07:09,360
hear from the people that were involved, but I do think we don't give credit enough to the individuals and often give credit to the entity that is Apple.

798
01:07:11,120 --> 01:07:20,080
And I do really like that because it is, it should be unsaid or usually is unsaid, but like that is a huge inspiration for what a lot of us are doing, right?

799
01:07:20,320 --> 01:07:22,880
Is the real work that they're doing all the time.

800
01:07:23,520 --> 01:07:23,920
Absolutely.

801
01:07:24,080 --> 01:07:28,640
And it has been, I mean, when I worked at Apple, this was very much endemic to the culture.

802
01:07:28,720 --> 01:07:38,800
It's just that Apple doesn't feel super comfortable with people taking individual credit and they're a part of the movement that's kind of breaking that open a little bit and are outspoken on Twitter and say like, yes, we contributed to this.

803
01:07:39,280 --> 01:07:40,160
We made this a success.

804
01:07:40,560 --> 01:07:42,480
And that cannot happen enough, honestly.

805
01:07:42,720 --> 01:07:51,360
We, Apple can graduate from that culture from the old Steve Jobs era of people not being able to take credit for their work because it's people that make this stuff.

806
01:07:51,520 --> 01:07:57,920
And like I said, the big problem with software often is that there's that big disconnect of this intangible thing coming from an intangible device.

807
01:07:58,400 --> 01:08:01,520
And you have to remember that people really put love and care into this.

808
01:08:02,080 --> 01:08:04,880
And really, really, they're very human, just like you.

809
01:08:05,600 --> 01:08:06,800
And it's good to name them.

810
01:08:07,120 --> 01:08:10,800
Good to name and also be kind to those people that do that.

811
01:08:11,200 --> 01:08:11,680
I like that.

812
01:08:13,360 --> 01:08:13,360
Ben.

813
01:08:14,240 --> 01:08:36,000
So I think on the iOS development side of things, I owe a ton to Lauren Brichter, who via the Tweety acquisition, I was able to, you know, I knew iOS development before, but being able to work with him on the iPhone app and also Twitter for Mac, it was like a crash course in, you know, how you as an indie developer ship really polished, beautiful products.

814
01:08:36,640 --> 01:08:39,840
And so, you know, it's invaluable, you know, everything I picked up from him.

815
01:08:40,480 --> 01:08:52,560
And taking a step back as far as, like, me as a developer, I think there are a lot of people who I learn from via their writing, like, you know, Jamie Zelinski, who, you know, he published a lot.

816
01:08:52,560 --> 01:08:57,440
He was around the Netscape era and while that company was being built.

817
01:08:57,520 --> 01:09:02,320
And he wrote really insightful articles like The Rise of Worse is Better.

818
01:09:03,040 --> 01:09:04,240
So like articles like that.

819
01:09:04,400 --> 01:09:07,120
Joel Spolsky wrote a bunch about shipping software.

820
01:09:07,680 --> 01:09:14,000
Peter Norvig, who's like he was head of AI research at Google, who's written really insightful posts.

821
01:09:14,160 --> 01:09:18,880
But I would say all these people who, you know, they weren't trying to sell you something.

822
01:09:19,280 --> 01:09:22,000
They just felt like they had useful information to share.

823
01:09:22,560 --> 01:09:25,600
And to some degree, we try to do that with what we're writing.

824
01:09:25,680 --> 01:09:28,800
I think it's like it's mostly about sharing stuff for the sake of sharing it.

825
01:09:29,280 --> 01:09:39,440
And so it's very rare to find find these people who are really incredible developers, really amazing at what they do, and they go out there and share this information with other people.

826
01:09:39,840 --> 01:09:44,000
It's like so philanthropic to, you know, learn from these people out there.

827
01:09:44,160 --> 01:09:46,960
And I hope that eventually we can give back in a similar way.

828
01:09:48,160 --> 01:09:53,760
And apart from that, you know, game developers are always fun to watch just seeing, like, how easy of a life we have.

829
01:09:54,000 --> 01:10:00,080
Just watching the GDC talks and be like, oh my God, like you spent three years on this system.

830
01:10:00,640 --> 01:10:06,160
And it's really humbling to see, like, the work that other people put into something they're super passionate about.

831
01:10:06,320 --> 01:10:07,360
And it's super inspiring.

832
01:10:07,680 --> 01:10:12,080
So just look at game developers to see how high a bar people hold themselves to.

833
01:10:12,960 --> 01:10:15,360
And yeah, I think that's that comes to mind.

834
01:10:15,920 --> 01:10:16,720
Man, I like that.

835
01:10:16,960 --> 01:10:17,360
I like it too.

836
01:10:18,400 --> 01:10:29,360
You started with Lauren, who's a frequently cited, but you being at Twitter and actually working with him, you more than almost anyone has probably been really deeply influenced by him.

837
01:10:29,760 --> 01:10:34,480
Yeah, it was, it was, so much of my life is just being really lucky and being at the right place at the right time.

838
01:10:35,840 --> 01:10:37,520
So that was an example there.

839
01:10:37,760 --> 01:10:46,640
And I guess, I don't know if, yeah, it's just like, I guess, I don't know if there's also a moral we want to give to your audience, but it wasn't until.

840
01:10:47,360 --> 01:10:52,000
ten or at least ten over ten years in my career that eventually I was able to do this.

841
01:10:52,560 --> 01:10:58,720
And I had so many fortunate experiences that led up to this time where I was able to work on the stuff that I actually want to work on.

842
01:10:59,280 --> 01:11:18,640
So I would say that, you know, if you don't hit what you're looking for the first time you try to ship an app, you know, there's no harm in learning from other people and spending years working in big companies, working in startups, whatever until you reach the point where you've had enough, you know, luck that you can make the leap.

843
01:11:18,960 --> 01:11:22,160
So, you know, just because, you know, we're in, we're lucky enough to be doing what we're doing.

844
01:11:22,320 --> 01:11:24,160
Don't hold yourself up to what we've done, you know.

845
01:11:26,080 --> 01:11:26,160
Yeah.

846
01:11:26,960 --> 01:11:28,400
I just wanted to echo that sentiment.

847
01:11:28,960 --> 01:11:31,360
That's a really, really, really powerful thing.

848
01:11:31,760 --> 01:11:38,320
I think Pilot was definitely a 10-year overnight success in the sense that we've looked and thought about this for a long time.

849
01:11:38,560 --> 01:11:46,480
But at the same time, the thing that we really want to put out there is, you know, a lot of people told us apps were, paid apps were dead.

850
01:11:46,880 --> 01:11:50,480
And, and, you know, it's impossible to do this and be successful in it.

851
01:11:50,640 --> 01:11:52,240
And, and, and, sure, it was, it's hard.

852
01:11:52,560 --> 01:11:56,880
I'm not going to say that it's easy if you just care that it just, you know, success shows up.

853
01:11:57,360 --> 01:12:02,800
But people very much appreciate, and there is still a giant shortage of apps made by people who truly care.

854
01:12:03,680 --> 01:12:12,800
And those that are created that way with passion, with, with, with, with, which, with, which cater so well to their niche, they just do really well.

855
01:12:13,120 --> 01:12:15,600
And Apple now also really highlights those.

856
01:12:15,840 --> 01:12:16,800
You know, that's the cool thing.

857
01:12:16,960 --> 01:12:18,320
Apple has this editorial process.

858
01:12:18,960 --> 01:12:21,840
They're very receptive to developers creating something special.

859
01:12:22,640 --> 01:12:32,080
And, um, if, if there's anyone listening that is, like, doubting if they should go and do that thing they're really passionate about, I'd say do it because there's, there's, it's a really good time for that.

860
01:12:32,560 --> 01:12:33,280
Well, wow.

861
01:12:33,440 --> 01:12:36,240
You guys, uh, cap that off like beautifully.

862
01:12:36,480 --> 01:12:39,040
So I think, I think we should, we should probably end it there.

863
01:12:39,360 --> 01:12:41,920
Uh, thank you both of so much for coming on.

864
01:12:42,240 --> 01:12:48,000
Like I said, HoloLife is in my, I mean, it's been in my dock since it came out basically.

865
01:12:48,960 --> 01:13:01,760
And very influential on me as far as just a software developer and then as I became an iOS developer and then at my, you know, scrambling attempts at being a UX designer.

866
01:13:02,000 --> 01:13:05,360
So I can't thank you enough for coming on.

867
01:13:05,520 --> 01:13:07,920
And I think everybody else is going to appreciate hearing that story.

868
01:13:08,240 --> 01:13:08,640
Awesome.

869
01:13:08,800 --> 01:13:09,440
Thanks for having us.

870
01:13:09,520 --> 01:13:10,240
Yeah, Charlie.

871
01:13:10,400 --> 01:13:11,440
Thank you so much for having us.

872
01:13:11,760 --> 01:13:12,720
It was an awesome interview.

873
